      "narrative": "The copper warmth of the Finch fades as you push through the door into the wet morning. Rain has slicked the cobbles to a dark sheen, and the air tastes of woodsmoke and wet stone. The street curves left, then opens onto a wide, empty square.\n\nAnd there it stands.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises from the flagstones like a broken tooth \u2014 a cracked black arch perhaps fifteen feet high, its surface pitted and scarred by weather no one remembers. It stands alone, no wall attached to either side, nothing to support it but the sheer weight of its own stone. Beyond it, through the gap where the gate's dark metal panel once slid, you can see only a grey, hazy stillness \u2014 as if the air itself refuses to move past the threshold.\n\nA figure sits at the base of the arch, hunched against the rain. Travel-stained cloak, hood drawn up. They don't seem to have noticed you yet \u2014 their attention is fixed on the gate's lower edge, where a scatter of tools lies across a scrap of oilcloth.\n\nThe rain patters against stone. The gate looms. The figure works in silence."
    },
