      "narrative": "Your voice cuts through the rain, calling the name of a dead man across an empty square. The name echoes briefly against the stone facades of nearby buildings, then fades into the grey hush.\n\nThe only answer is the wet hush of rain on cobbles.\n\nThe hooded figure at the base of the arch stops working. Their hands go still over the scattered tools. For a long moment they don't move at all \u2014 just a hunched shape in the rain, head cocked slightly as if listening.\n\nThen they straighten, slow and deliberate, and turn.\n\nThe hood shadows their face, but you catch the glint of an eye catching the grey light. A woman's voice, rough as rusted iron, cuts through the rain.\n\n\"Bram's been dead near a year now, stranger. You'll get no answer from that direction.\"\n\nShe watches you from beneath the hood, one hand resting on a wrench laid across her knee. She doesn't rise, doesn't retreat \u2014 just sits there, measuring you with her silence."
