      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch, scanning the ground beneath the arch \u2014 the rain-darkened flagstones, the grit and loose gravel that's accumulated at the base. Your fingers brush the wet stone. Nothing. Just rubble and dirt and the grey smear of old weather. If something was hidden here, it's long gone, or buried deeper than a casual search turns up.\n\nThe woman watches you scrape at nothing. A long, slow breath escapes her.\n\n\"Looking for ghosts, are you?\" Her voice carries no mockery, only a flat weariness. \"Bram's secrets went with him. I've picked through every crack in these stones for months. You won't find what isn't there.\"\n\nShe shifts, the oilcloth rustling beneath her tools. The rain continues its steady drum against the arch.\n\n\"So. You called for a dead man. You're scratching at bare stone. What exactly are you hoping to find at a gate that hasn't opened in seventeen years?\""
