      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans shut behind you, sealing out the wet grey morning. The tavern is quieter now \u2014 the breakfast crowd has thinned, leaving only a few scattered patrons nursing cups by the windows. Your old table sits empty, the scarred wood still bearing the ring-marks of countless mugs.\n\nYou settle into the seat. The wood groans familiarly. The fire crackles low in the hearth.\n\nA moment later, a figure detaches from the bar \u2014 Mara Venn, still in her charcoal coat, two steaming cups in hand. She crosses without asking, sets one down across from you, and takes the opposite seat.\n\n\"Didn't find what you were looking for, I take it.\" Not a question. She wraps her fingers around her cup, studying you. \"I saw you leave. Saw you come back. The Ash Gate doesn't give much to anyone who doesn't know where to look.\"\n\nShe takes a sip. The fire pops. Through the window, the rain continues its steady fall.\n\n\"Is it the gate you're after, or what's on the other side?\""
